The '''2013 FA Cup final''' was an [[association football]] match between [[Manchester City F.C.|Manchester City]] and [[Wigan Athletic F.C.|Wigan Athletic]] on 11 May 2013 at [[Wembley Stadium]] in [[London]], England, organised by [[the Football Association]] (FA). It marked the [[List of FA Cup Finals|132nd final]] of the [[Football Association Challenge Cup]] (FA Cup), the world's oldest football cup competition. It was Wigan's first FA Cup final and Manchester City's tenth. En route to the final, Manchester City defeated [[Watford F.C.|Watford]], [[Stoke City F.C.|Stoke City]], [[Leeds United F.C.|Leeds United]], [[Barnsley F.C.|Barnsley]] and [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]]; Wigan Athletic beat [[AFC Bournemouth|Bournemouth]] (after a [[Replay (sports)|replay]]), [[Macclesfield Town F.C.|Macclesfield Town]], [[Huddersfield Town A.F.C.|Huddersfield Town]], [[Everton F.C.|Everton]] and [[Millwall F.C.|Millwall]].

The match [[Kick-off (association football)|kicked off]] in the early evening in front of 86,254 spectators and was [[Referee (association football)|refereed]] by [[Andre Marriner]]. After a goalless first half, Manchester City's [[Pablo Zabaleta]] was [[sent off]] for a second [[Penalty card#Yellow card|yellow card]], becoming the third player to be sent off in an FA Cup final. One minute into [[injury time]], Wigan Athletic won a [[corner kick]] which was taken by [[Shaun Maloney]]: [[Ben Watson (footballer, born July 1985)|Ben Watson]] outjumped [[Jack Rodwell]] and [[Header (association football)|headed]] the ball over [[Joe Hart]], the Manchester City goalkeeper, and into the goal to secure a 1&ndash;0 win. The cup was jointly lifted by playing captain [[Emmerson Boyce]] and club captain [[Gary Caldwell]]. Wigan Athletic's [[Callum McManaman]] was named as [[Player of the match|man of the match]].

Manchester City and their semi-final opponent Chelsea had already qualified for the [[2013–14 UEFA Champions League|following season's Champions League]] by virtue of the clubs' league positions. Wigan Athletic secured qualification for the [[2013–14 UEFA Europa League|2013–14 Europa League]] competition after they had won their semi-final against Millwall. Two days after the final, Manchester City sacked manager [[Roberto Mancini]], confirming rumours of his fate before the match. Three days later, Wigan Athletic were [[Promotion and relegation|relegated]] from the Premier League following a 4–1 loss to [[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]], becoming the first club to win the FA Cup and be relegated from the highest tier of English football in the same season.

==Background==
The world's oldest football cup competition,<ref name=finals>{{Cite web |url=http://www.thefa.com/competitions/thefacup/more/finals |publisher=[[The Football Association]] |title=FA Cup Finals, 1872 – today |access-date=9 July 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170613182709/http://www.thefa.com/competitions/thefacup/more/finals |archive-date=13 June 2017 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="ArsenalManUtdChelseaLiverpool">{{Cite web |url=https://www.fourfourtwo.com/features/who-has-won-most-fa-cups-12-most-successful-clubs-arsenal-man-utd-chelsea-liverpool |work=[[FourFourTwo]] |access-date=5 October 2020 |title=Who has won the most FA Cups? The 12 most successful clubs in the world's oldest competition |first=Sam |last=Blitz |date=3 August 2020 |archive-date=5 October 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201005103153/https://www.fourfourtwo.com/features/who-has-won-most-fa-cups-12-most-successful-clubs-arsenal-man-utd-chelsea-liverpool |url-status=live }}</ref> the [[FA Cup]] is an annual [[knockout tournament]] involving professional and amateur men's [[association football|football]] clubs in the [[English football league system]].{{sfn|O'Leary|2017|p=63}} The final of the [[2012–13 FA Cup]] was the 132nd to be played since the tournament was first held in 1872.<ref name=finals/> [[Manchester City F.C.|Manchester City]] were making their tenth appearance in an FA Cup final since the club was founded in 1880.<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.mancity.com/club/manchester-city-history | publisher = [[Manchester City F.C.]] | access-date = 4 October 2021 | title = Manchester City history | archive-date = 4 October 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20211004083942/https://www.mancity.com/club/manchester-city-history | url-status = live }}</ref> They had won the cup five times (in [[1904 FA Cup final|1904]], [[1934 FA Cup final|1934]], [[1956 FA Cup final|1956]], [[1969 FA Cup final|1969]] and [[2011 FA Cup final|2011]]) and were runners-up four times ([[1926 FA Cup final|1926]], [[1933 FA Cup final|1933]], [[1955 FA Cup final|1955]] and [[1981 FA Cup final|1981]]).<ref name=fafinals/> Conversely, [[Wigan Athletic F.C.|Wigan Athletic]] were making their first appearance in an FA Cup Final since the club was founded in 1932.<ref name=fafinals>{{cite web |title=Cup Final Results |url=http://www.thefa.com/Competitions/FACompetitions/TheFACup/History/cupfinalresults |publisher=[[The Football Association]] |access-date=15 April 2013 |archive-date=10 July 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140710171239/http://www.thefa.com/Competitions/FACompetitions/TheFACup/History/cupfinalresults |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web | url = https://wiganathletic.com/club/club-history/ | publisher = [[Wigan Athletic F.C.]] | access-date = 4 October 2021 | title = Club history | archive-date = 4 October 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20211004151002/https://wiganathletic.com/club/club-history/ | url-status = live }}</ref>

Manchester City had won both [[Premier League]] games between the sides during [[2012–13 Premier League|the regular season]]. They secured a 2–0 victory at the [[DW Stadium]] in November 2012 with second-half goals from [[Mario Balotelli]] and [[James Milner]] and a 1–0 win the following April at the [[City of Manchester Stadium]] after a late goal from [[Carlos Tevez]].<ref>{{Cite web| url = https://www.11v11.com/matches/wigan-athletic-v-manchester-city-28-november-2012-299667/| website = 11v11.com | publisher = Association of Football Statisticians| title = Wigan Athletic v Manchester City, 28 November 2012| access-date = 4 October 2021| archive-date = 27 June 2021| archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210627195252/https://www.11v11.com/matches/wigan-athletic-v-manchester-city-28-november-2012-299667/| url-status = live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.11v11.com/matches/manchester-city-v-wigan-athletic-17-april-2013-301210/ | website = 11v11.com | publisher = Association of Football Statisticians | title = Manchester City v Wigan Athletic, 17 April 2013 | access-date = 4 October 2021 | archive-date = 28 June 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210628222445/https://www.11v11.com/matches/manchester-city-v-wigan-athletic-17-april-2013-301210/ | url-status = live }}</ref> The final was being played before the last two games of the Premier League season,<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.11v11.com/teams/wigan-athletic/tab/matches/season/2013/ | website = 11v11.com | publisher = Association of Football Statisticians | access-date = 8 October 2021 | title = Wigan Athletic football club match record: 2013 | archive-date = 30 January 2013 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20130130182110/http://www.11v11.com/teams/wigan-athletic/tab/matches/season/2013 | url-status = live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.11v11.com/teams/manchester-city/tab/matches/season/2013/ | website = 11v11.com | publisher = Association of Football Statisticians | access-date = 8 October 2021 | title = Wigan Athletic football club match record: 2013 | archive-date = 13 December 2013 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20131213011409/http://www.11v11.com/teams/manchester-city/tab/matches/season/2013 | url-status = live }}</ref> with Wigan Athletic in eighteenth place in the league, 40 points behind Manchester City in second position.<ref>{{Cite web| url = https://www.11v11.com/league-tables/premier-league/10-may-2013/| title = Premier League table after close of play on 10 May 2013| website = 11v11.com | publisher = Association of Football Statisticians| access-date = 8 October 2021| archive-date = 8 October 2021| archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20211008105031/https://www.11v11.com/league-tables/premier-league/10-may-2013/| url-status = live}}</ref> Manchester City had beaten Wigan Athletic in each of the most recent seven Premier League matches between the sides.<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/22491410 | title = FA Cup final preview: Manchester City v Wigan | access-date = 13 October 2021 | date = 11 May 2013 | work= [[BBC Sport]] | archive-date = 13 October 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20211013102002/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/22491410 | url-status = live }}</ref> City had also beaten Wigan in both of their previous FA Cup ties; two 1–0 home wins back in [[1970–71 FA Cup|1971]] and [[2005–06 FA Cup|2006]].<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.11v11.com/teams/manchester-city/tab/opposingTeams/opposition/Wigan%20Athletic/ | website = 11v11.com | publisher = Association of Football Statisticians | access-date = 22 June 2021 | title = Manchester City football club: record v Wigan Athletic | archive-date = 26 September 2019 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20190926194236/https://www.11v11.com/teams/manchester-city/tab/opposingTeams/opposition/Wigan%20Athletic/ | url-status = live }}</ref>

Wigan Athletic's leading scorer during the regular season was [[Arouna Koné]] who had 13 goals, 11 in the league and two in the FA Cup.<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.soccerbase.com/teams/team.sd?team_id=2783&teamTabs=stats&season_id=142 | website = Soccerbase | publisher = Centurycomm | title = Wigan squad details – 2012/2013 – Player appearances | access-date = 23 June 2021 | archive-date = 11 April 2016 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20160411061830/http://www.soccerbase.com/teams/team.sd?season_id=142&team_id=2783&teamTabs=stats | url-status = live }}</ref> [[Sergio Agüero]] led the scoring for Manchester City with 15 goals during the season, including 3 in the FA Cup.<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.soccerbase.com/teams/team.sd?team_id=1718&teamTabs=stats&season_id=142 | website = Soccerbase | publisher = Centurycomm | title = Man City squad details – 2012/2013 – Player appearances | access-date = 23 June 2021 | archive-date = 4 October 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20211004064708/https://www.soccerbase.com/teams/team.sd?team_id=1718&teamTabs=stats&season_id=142 | url-status = live }}</ref>

