Premier League side Wigan Athletic's FA Cup campaign also began in the third round, in which they faced [[EFL League One|League One]] side [[AFC Bournemouth]] at the DW Stadium. [[Roberto Martínez]] made nine changes to the team that had played in the league four days earlier.<ref>{{cite web |first=Si |last=Hughes |title=Wigan Athletic 1 Bournemouth 1: match report |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/competitions/fa-cup/9780874/Wigan-Athletic-1-Bournemouth-1-match-report.html |work=[[The Daily Telegraph]]| url-access=subscription |date=5 January 2013 |access-date=15 April 2013 |archive-date=8 January 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130108235246/http://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/competitions/fa-cup/9780874/Wigan-Athletic-1-Bournemouth-1-match-report.html |url-status=live }}</ref> [[Eunan O'Kane]] scored for the visiting side towards the end of the first half with a strike from distance. Wigan Athletic hit the Bournemouth crossbar twice in the second half before they won a penalty after [[Simon Francis (footballer)|Simon Francis]] fouled [[Maynor Figueroa]] in the area in the 70th minute. [[Shwan Jalal]] saved [[Jordi Gómez]]'s penalty kick but Gómez scored from the rebound to make it 1–1, and ensured the tie would need to be settled in a [[Replay (sports)|replay]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Wigan 1–1 Bournemouth |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/20863580 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=5 January 2013 |access-date=9 May 2013 |archive-date=8 January 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130108064138/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/20863580 |url-status=live }}</ref>  At [[Dean Court]] ten days later, the match was settled by a single goal: in the first half, Bournemouth's [[Harry Arter]] conceded possession of the ball to [[Mauro Boselli]] whose shot from around {{convert|20|yd}} flew into the top corner of the goal, securing a 1–0 win for Wigan Athletic.<ref>{{cite news |first=Nabil |last=Hassan |title=Bournemouth 0–1 Wigan |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/20942524 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=15 January 2013 |access-date=9 May 2013 |archive-date=1 April 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130401185235/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/20942524 |url-status=live }}</ref> They were drawn against [[Non-League football|non-league]] team [[Macclesfield Town F.C.|Macclesfield Town]] in the fourth round. To avoid postponement, snow had to be cleared from the pitch at [[Moss Rose]] on the morning of the game. Six minutes into the match, [[Thierry Audel]] fouled [[Callum McManaman]] in the Macclesfield [[penalty area]] and Gómez scored the resulting penalty to give Wigan Athletic the lead. The home side had several opportunities to score, including a header from Audel from {{convert|6|yd}} which went straight to Wigan Athletic's debutant goalkeeper [[Joel Robles]]. Macclesfield's appeals for a penalty late in the match were turned down by the referee and Wigan Athletic won 1–0. Victory gave them their second appearance in the fifth round of the FA Cup since the founding of the club.<ref>{{cite web |first=Chris |last=Bevan |title=Macclesfield 0–1 Wigan |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/21096016 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=26 January 2013 |access-date=16 April 2013 |archive-date=17 February 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130217071503/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/21096016 |url-status=live }}</ref>

In the fifth round, Wigan Athletic were drawn away against Championship team [[Huddersfield Town A.F.C.|Huddersfield Town]]. McManaman gave the visiting side the lead in the 31st minute, shooting into the top corner of the goal after a pass from [[James McArthur]]. Five minutes before half-time, Koné doubled his side's lead after Gómez had passed from a McManaman cross before McArthur made it 3–0 when he scored in the 56th minute. [[Lee Novak]] scored with a header from a [[Calum Woods]] cross to reduce Huddersfield Town's deficit but with a minute of the match remaining, Koné scored his second to secure a 4–1 win for Wigan Athletic.<ref>{{cite web |first=Alistair |last=Magowan |title=Huddersfield 1–4 Wigan |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/21448831 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=17 February 2013 |access-date=9 May 2013 |archive-date=2 April 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130402084848/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/21448831 |url-status=live }}</ref> Their quarter-final opponents were Premier League side [[Everton F.C.|Everton]] whom they faced at [[Goodison Park]]. Wigan Athletic scored three goals within four minutes in the first half: just after half an hour, Wigan Athletic's Figueroa opened the scoring when he headed in a corner. McManaman then doubled the lead when a mistake by [[Phil Neville]] allowed him to take the ball past Everton's goalkeeper [[Ján Mucha]] and shoot. Gómez then struck a long-range shot into the Everton goal to make it 3–0, the final score. The win ensured Wigan Athletic would play in the first FA Cup semi-final appearance in the club's history.<ref>{{cite web |first=Phil |last=McNulty |authorlink=Phil McNulty |title=Everton 0–3 Wigan |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/21639357 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=9 March 2013 |access-date=9 May 2013 |archive-date=14 April 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130414204655/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/21639357 |url-status=live }}</ref>

Wigan Athletic's fifth appearance at Wembley Stadium saw them face Championship side [[Millwall F.C.|Millwall]] in the semi-final. In the first half, Koné's cross found [[Shaun Maloney]] who volleyed the ball past [[David Forde (footballer)|David Forde]] in the Millwall goal to give Wigan Athletic the lead. With twelve minutes of the match remaining, Wigan Athletic broke out of defence and Gómez's pass found McManaman who took the ball round Forde to double his side's lead. Wigan Athletic won the match 2–0 and progressed to the first FA Cup final in the club's history.<ref>{{cite web |first=Phil |last=McNulty |authorlink=Phil McNulty |title=Millwall 0–2 Wigan |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/22049714 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=13 April 2013 |access-date=9 May 2013 |archive-date=22 April 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130422225000/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/22049714 |url-status=live }}</ref>

==Match==

===Pre-match===
[[File:Roberto Mancini - Lech - Manchester 005 (cropped).jpg|thumb|upright|alt=Roberto Mancini|Manchester City's manager Roberto Mancini's position was a matter of speculation before the final.]]
[[File:Opening ceremony FA Cup Final 2013.jpg|thumb|alt=A football stadium with scores of perfomers and smoke machines on the feild|Musical and pyrotechnic performance before the match]]
Before the match, it was reported in ''[[The Daily Telegraph]]'' that manager [[Roberto Mancini]]'s job was under threat after he failed to lead Manchester City to a defence of their league title. It was suggested that the [[Málaga CF|Málaga]] manager [[Manuel Pellegrini]] was to be Mancini's successor.<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/teams/manchester-city/10050337/Manchester-City-manager-Roberto-Mancini-on-the-brink-as-club-close-in-on-Malagas-Manuel-Pellegrini.html | work = [[The Daily Telegraph]] | title = Manchester City manager Roberto Mancini on the brink as club close in on Malaga's Manuel Pellegrini | access-date = 24 June 2021 | date = 10 May 2013 | first1 = Mark | last1 = Ogden | first2 = Chris | last2 = Bascombe | authorlink2=Chris Bascombe| archive-date = 12 November 2020 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20201112032040/http://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/teams/manchester-city/10050337/Manchester-City-manager-Roberto-Mancini-on-the-brink-as-club-close-in-on-Malagas-Manuel-Pellegrini.html | url-status = live | url-access=subscription}}</ref> The [[Referee (association football)|referee]] for the match was [[Andre Marriner]] who had officiated at Wembley Stadium twice previously, at the [[2010 FA Community Shield]] and the [[2010 Football League Championship play-off final]]. He was [[Assistant referee (association football)|assisted]] by Stephen Child and Simon Long, [[Anthony Taylor (referee)|Anthony Taylor]] was the [[fourth official]] and Gary Beswick acted as the [[reserve assistant referee]].<ref name="referee"/>

Manchester City were considered strong favourites to win the match by the British media.<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://link.gale.com/apps/doc/A329352878/STND?u=wikipedia&sid=bookmark-STND&xid=c5353c8d | work = [[The Guardian]] | first = Jamie | last = Jackson | url-access = subscription | via = [[Gale (publisher)|Gale]] | date = 11 May 2013 | access-date = 24 June 2021 | title = Manchester City v Wigan Athletic Today 5.15pm ESPN & ITV | page = 10 | archive-date = 25 June 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210625101138/https://go.gale.com/ps/i.do?p=STND&u=wikipedia&id=GALE%7CA329352878&v=2.1&it=r&sid=bookmark-STND&asid=c5353c8d | url-status = live }}</ref><ref name=teleprev>{{Cite web | url = https://link.gale.com/apps/doc/A329328855/STND?u=wikipedia&sid=bookmark-STND&xid=fdb1f1d9 | work = [[The Daily Telegraph]] | first = John | last = Ley | url-access = subscription | via = [[Gale (publisher)|Gale]] | date = 11 May 2013 | access-date = 24 June 2021 | title = Manchester City v Wigan Athletic: FA Cup final match preview | page = 10 | archive-date = 24 June 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210624195139/https://go.gale.com/ps/i.do?p=STND&u=wikipedia&id=GALE%7CA329328855&v=2.1&it=r&sid=bookmark-STND&asid=fdb1f1d9 | url-status = live }}</ref> Wigan Athletic wore the club's black away [[Kit (association football)|kit]] for the final, used the away team dressing room and were allocated the East End of the stadium. Manchester City fans occupied the West End and the team played in their home kit after winning the coin toss to decide who would wear which kit.<ref>{{cite web |title=Latics in black for final |url=http://www.wiganlatics.co.uk/news/article/13-04-16-latics-in-black-for-final-775583.aspx |publisher=[[Wigan Athletic F.C.]] |date=16 April 2013 |access-date=17 April 2013 |archive-date=20 April 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130420020138/http://www.wiganlatics.co.uk/news/article/13-04-16-latics-in-black-for-final-775583.aspx |url-status=live }}</ref> Ticket prices for the final started at £45 and were available at £65, £85 and £115, with a £10 discount for concessions.<ref>{{cite web |title=2013 FA Cup Final Ticket Prices |url=http://www.thefa.com/TheFACup/News/2013/Apr/cup-final-ticket-prices-170413.aspx |publisher=[[The Football Association]] |date=17 April 2013 |access-date=24 April 2013 |archive-date=19 April 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140419020600/http://www.thefa.com/TheFACup/News/2013/Apr/cup-final-ticket-prices-170413.aspx |url-status=live }}</ref> Manchester City received an initial allocation of 25,000 tickets, later increased to 31,779, and Wigan Athletic requested 21,000 tickets, later increased to a 25,000 allocation.<ref>{{cite news |title=FA Cup Final – Updated ticket information |url=http://www.mcfc.co.uk/News/Tickets-and-Travel/2013/April/FA-Cup-final-ticket-update-III |publisher=[[Manchester City F.C.]] |date=3 May 2013 |access-date=24 April 2013 |archive-date=5 May 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130505233456/http://www.mcfc.co.uk/News/Tickets-and-Travel/2013/April/FA-Cup-final-ticket-update-III |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=FA Cup Final Ticket Details |url=http://www.wiganlatics.co.uk/news/article/13-04-17-ticket-details-fa-cup-final-776729.aspx |publisher= [[Wigan Athletic F.C.]] |date=17 April 2013 |access-date=24 April 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130420034930/http://www.wiganlatics.co.uk/news/article/13-04-17-ticket-details-fa-cup-final-776729.aspx |archive-date=20 April 2013 |url-status=dead |df=dmy-all }}</ref>

Despite expectations in the media that Pantilimon would retain his position as the Manchester City goalkeeper for the final, he was dropped to the substitute's bench in place of [[Joe Hart]].<ref name=teleprev/><ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/22475694 | title = FA Cup final: Man City's Costel Pantilimon set to get big chance | first = Chris | last = Bevan | date = 10 May 2013 | publisher = [[BBC Sport]] | access-date = 24 June 2021 | archive-date = 24 May 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210524205214/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/22475694 | url-status = live }}</ref> Before the match, Mancini said "I decided this because I wanted this for this game but it doesn't change anything{{nbsp}}... We know Costel is a good keeper."<ref name=guardasit/> Manchester City had no injury problems, Touré being included in the team after suffering from a muscle problem.<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.theguardian.com/football/2013/may/10/manchester-city-wigan-athletic-squad-sheets | work = [[The Guardian]] | access-date = 6 October 2021 | title = Manchester City v Wigan Athletic: squad sheets | first = Jamie | last = Jackson | date = 10 May 2013 | archive-date = 4 October 2015 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20151004083155/http://www.theguardian.com/football/2013/may/10/manchester-city-wigan-athletic-squad-sheets | url-status = live }}</ref><ref name=yaya>{{Cite web | url = https://www.theguardian.com/football/2013/may/06/yaya-toure-injury-manchester-city | work = [[The Guardian]] | access-date = 6 October 2021 | title = Yaya Touré should be fit for FA Cup final despite muscle fatigue | first = Jamie | last = Jackson | date = 6 May 2013 | archive-date = 6 October 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20211006103523/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2013/may/06/yaya-toure-injury-manchester-city | url-status = live }}</ref> Manchester City made eight changes to the starting line-up from their previous match, a Premier League game against [[West Bromwich Albion F.C.|West Bromwich]]; only Hart, Nasri and Tevez keeping their place.<ref name=guardasit/><ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.11v11.com/matches/manchester-city-v-west-bromwich-albion-07-may-2013-301371/ | title = Manchester City v West Bromwich Albion, 07 May 2013 | website = 11v11.com | publisher = Association of Football Statisticians| access-date = 13 October 2021 | archive-date = 9 October 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20211009125947/https://www.11v11.com/matches/manchester-city-v-west-bromwich-albion-07-may-2013-301371/ | url-status = live }}</ref> [[Antolín Alcaraz]] returned to the Wigan Athletic starting lineup after recovering from a [[Pulled hamstring|torn hamstring]] in place of [[Gary Caldwell]], and Figueroa was ruled out with a groin injury.<ref name=guardasit/><ref name=yaya/><ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.espn.co.uk/football/news/story/_/id/1566119/everton-defender-antolin-alcaraz-suffers-new-injury-setback | publisher = [[ESPN]] | access-date = 6 October 2021 | title = Antolin Alcaraz suffers new setback | first = Mike | last = Whalley | date = 28 September 2013 | archive-date = 14 December 2019 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20191214124247/https://www.espn.co.uk/football/news/story/_/id/1566119/everton-defender-antolin-alcaraz-suffers-new-injury-setback | url-status = live }}</ref><ref name=wiganprev>{{Cite web | url = https://www.11v11.com/matches/wigan-athletic-v-swansea-city-07-may-2013-301372/ | website = 11v11.com | publisher = Association of Football Statisticians | access-date = 13 October 2021 | title = Wigan Athletic v Swansea City, 07 May 2013 | archive-date = 9 October 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20211009130840/https://www.11v11.com/matches/wigan-athletic-v-swansea-city-07-may-2013-301372/ | url-status = live }}</ref> [[Ben Watson (footballer, born July 1985)|Ben Watson]] dropped to the substitute's bench and was replaced by Gómez.<ref name=guardasit/><ref name=wiganprev/> Wigan Athletic adopted a 3–4–1–2 formation,<ref name=":1">{{cite web | url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/43086897 | title=FA Cup: The inside story of Wigan's win over Manchester City in the 2013 final | work=BBC Sport | date=17 February 2018 }}</ref> and Manchester City played as a [[Formation (association football)#4–2–3–1|4–2–3–1]].<ref name=timesrep>{{Cite news | url = https://link.gale.com/apps/doc/EIKOLA889006626/TTDA?u=wikipedia&sid=bookmark-TTDA&xid=4ee18ea2 | work = [[The Times]] | pages = 90–91 | via = [[Gale (publisher)|Gale]] | url-access = subscription | title = Wigan's fairytale triumph rekindles fading romance | first = Oliver | last = Kay | date = 13 May 2013 | access-date = 24 June 2021 | url-status = live | archive-date = 25 June 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210625101305/https://go.gale.com/ps/i.do?p=TTDA&u=wikipedia&id=GALE%7CEIKOLA889006626&v=2.1&it=r&sid=bookmark-TTDA&asid=4ee18ea2 }}</ref>

The kick-off time for the final was 5:15{{nbsp}}p.m. as opposed to the traditional 3{{nbsp}}p.m. This was controversial, particularly with regard to both sets of supporters facing difficulties in guaranteeing train transport back to the North West of England after the match.<ref name=times/> The secretary of the Manchester City Supporters Club stated that "Not only does it show that [the FA] don't care but it shows that they don't know", and a spokesperson for the Wigan Athletic Supporters Club noted that "The FA don't think about stuff like young children of four or six years old potentially not getting home till 2{{nbsp}}a.m. if they are travelling by coach."<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.theguardian.com/football/2013/apr/17/manchester-city-wigan-fans-cup-final | work = [[The Guardian]] | access-date = 8 October 2021 | title = Manchester City and Wigan fans slam FA over train travel claim | first = James | last = Riach | date = 17 April 2013 | archive-date = 21 October 2015 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20151021192447/http://www.theguardian.com/football/2013/apr/17/manchester-city-wigan-fans-cup-final | url-status = live }}</ref> It was also the subject of a cross-party [[early day motion]] in the [[House of Commons of the United Kingdom|House of Commons]].<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://edm.parliament.uk/early-day-motion/45470/fa-cup-final | title = FA Cup | access-date = 8 October 2021 | publisher = [[Parliament of the United Kingdom|UK Parliament]] | archive-date = 8 October 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20211008145325/https://edm.parliament.uk/early-day-motion/45470/fa-cup-final | url-status = live }}</ref>

Wigan Athletic owner [[Dave Whelan]] led out his team before kick-off along with manager Martínez.<ref name=guardasit>{{Cite web | url = https://www.theguardian.com/football/2013/may/11/manchester-city-wigan-fa-cup-final-live-mbm | work = [[The Guardian]] | access-date = 24 June 2021 | title = Manchester City v Wigan: FA Cup final – as it happened | first = Jacob | last = Steinberg | date = 11 May 2013 | archive-date = 19 January 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210119094319/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2013/may/11/manchester-city-wigan-fa-cup-final-live-mbm | url-status = live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |first=Tom |last=Rostance |title=2013 FA Cup Final: As it happened |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/22485085 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=11 May 2013 |access-date=15 May 2013 |archive-date=11 June 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130611065150/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/22485085 |url-status=dead }}</ref> The traditional pre-match anthem, "[[Abide with Me]]", was performed by musical quartet Amore alongside the [[Royal Philharmonic Orchestra|Royal Philharmonic Concert Orchestra]]. The [[God Save the Queen|national anthem]] was also performed by Amore with the [[Band of the Grenadier Guards]].<ref>{{cite web |title=2013 FA Cup Final day schedule |url=http://www.thefa.com/TheFACup/News/2013/May/the-fa-cup-final-match-info.aspx |publisher=[[The Football Association]] |date=11 May 2013 |access-date=15 May 2013 |archive-date=11 June 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130611104556/http://www.thefa.com/TheFACup/News/2013/May/the-fa-cup-final-match-info.aspx |url-status=live }}</ref> Paralympic footballer [[David Clarke (Paralympic footballer)|Dave Clarke]] and [[David Bernstein (executive)|David Bernstein]], chairman of the FA, were introduced to the teams prior to [[Kick-off (association football)|kick-off]].<ref name=guardasit/> In the United Kingdom, the match was televised by [[ITV (TV network)|ITV]] and [[BT Sport ESPN|ESPN]].<ref name="referee"/>

===Summary===
====First half====
Wigan Athletic kicked off the match around 5:15{{nbsp}}p.m. in front of a crowd of 86,254.<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.11v11.com/matches/manchester-city-v-wigan-athletic-11-may-2013-301401/ | title = Manchester City v Wigan Athletic, 11 May 2013 | website=11v11.com | publisher = Association of Football Statisticians | access-date = 25 June 2021 | archive-date = 20 April 2017 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20170420073023/http://www.11v11.com/matches/manchester-city-v-wigan-athletic-11-may-2013-301401/ | url-status = live }}</ref> Two minutes into the game, Zabaleta made a run down the right wing and crossed the ball, which took a deflection before falling to Silva whose volley was blocked by McArthur. Agüero was then fouled by Alcaraz on the edge of the Wigan Athletic penalty area but Tevez's subsequent free kick was blocked with the ball finding Touré whose shot was pushed away by Robles. In the ninth minute, Wigan Athletic's McManaman made a run down the right-hand side of the pitch after a pass from Koné. He cut inside and struck the ball from around {{convert|8|yd|0}}, but his shot went wide of the Manchester City post. Wigan Athletic then enjoyed a spell of pressure before Silva's 18th-minute shot for Manchester City was blocked. Midway through the half, Maloney's shot from around {{convert|30|yd}} was off-target before [[Matija Nastasić]] failed to score with a long-range strike for Manchester City. In the 29th minute, Silva passed the ball into the Wigan Athletic penalty area and found Tevez, who stretched to shoot, but Robles made a save with his boot. Tevez's shot from the corner of the Wigan Athletic penalty area was too high before Gomez's strike from {{convert|25|yd}} was blocked by Kompany. With nine minutes of the half remaining, Alcaraz ran through the middle of the pitch and passed to McManaman who went round Nastasić, before his shot was blocked by Zabaleta. Nasri's cross was then headed off-target by Silva. In the 41st minute, Zabaleta's shot was blocked but the ball fell to Barry whose curling strike was saved by Robles. Just before half-time, Robles saved Nasri's {{convert|20|yd|adj=on}} shot and the half ended 0–0.<ref name=guardasit/>

====Second half====
[[File:Play during the FA Cup Final 2013.jpg|thumb|alt=A packed football stadium with overcast sky in the 63rd minute|Play during the second half of the match]]
Neither side made any changes to their playing personnel during the interval and Manchester City kicked off the second half. Two minutes in, McCarthy's shot was wide before Agüero's attempt to score was defended by [[Emmerson Boyce]]. A corner from Barry was then headed onto the roof of the Wigan Athletic net by Kompany. In the 54th minute, Manchester City made the first [[Substitute (association football)|substitution]] of the game with Milner coming on to replace Nasri. On the hour mark, Zabaleta became the first player of the match to be shown the [[Penalty card#Yellow card|yellow card]] after he committed a [[professional foul]] on McManaman who had made a break for Wigan Athletic. In the 64th minute, Gomez's shot from {{convert|25|yd}} went over the Manchester City crossbar before Kompany tackled McManaman after the Wigan Athletic player had [[dribbling|dribbled]] past both Silva and [[Gaël Clichy]]. Five minutes later, [[Jack Rodwell]] was brought on in place of Tevez in Manchester City's second change of the game. In the 72nd minute, [[Roger Espinoza]] fouled Milner who took the resulting free kick himself, from which Rodwell's header was straight at Robles. Nastasić was then booked for a foul on McManaman before Maloney's cross struck the Manchester City crossbar. In the 81st minute, Wigan Athletic made their first substitution, with Ben Watson coming on to replace Gomez. Three minutes later, Kompany received a poor pass from Clichy in the centre circle which Koné intercepted.  He passed to McManaman who was fouled by Zabaleta around {{convert|30|yd}} from the Manchester City goal. As a result, Zabaleta was [[sent off]] after receiving a second booking, and became the third player to be dismissed in an FA Cup final. Barry was then shown the yellow card for bringing down Maloney. In the final minute of the match, Wigan Athletic won a corner which was played in by Maloney. Watson beat Rodwell to the ball at the near post and headed it inside the far post of the goal to give Wigan Athletic the lead. Three minutes into stoppage time, Robles was booked for time-wasting and Manchester City brought on [[Edin Džeko]] for Barry. A minute later, the final whistle was blown and Wigan Athletic had won the match 1–0, securing the FA Cup for the first time in their history.<ref name=guardasit/>

