==Post-match==
[[File:David Sharpe, Paul Sharpe and David Whelan holding FA cup.png|thumb|Members of the Wigan Athletic board holding the [[FA Cup]] trophy following their team's victory]]

The cup was jointly lifted by playing captain Boyce and club captain Caldwell.<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/fa-league-cups/fa-cup-final-take-place-after-end-premier-league-season-8664458.html | work = [[The Independent]] | access-date = 24 June 2021 | title = FA Cup final to take place after the end of the Premier League season | first = Simon | last = Stone | date = 19 June 2013 | url-access = registration | archive-date = 24 June 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210624195132/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/fa-league-cups/fa-cup-final-take-place-after-end-premier-league-season-8664458.html | url-status = live }}</ref> Wigan Athletic's McManaman was named [[Player of the match|man of the match]].<ref name="man of the match">{{cite web |first=Nicholas |last=Veevers |title=Cup wonder for Wigan |url=http://www.thefa.com/TheFACup/News/2013/May/man-city-wigan-final-report.aspx |publisher=[[The Football Association]] |date=11 May 2013 |access-date=12 May 2013 |archive-date=7 June 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130607155730/http://www.thefa.com/TheFACup/News/2013/May/man-city-wigan-final-report.aspx |url-status=live }}</ref> On 20 May, Wigan Athletic had an open-top bus tour of the town to celebrate the FA Cup victory.<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-england-manchester-22598260 | title = Wigan Athletic in FA Cup bus parade | date = 20 May 2013 | access-date = 24 June 2021 | work= [[BBC News]] | archive-date = 10 August 2013 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20130810123450/http://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-england-manchester-22598260 | url-status = live }}</ref> Zabaleta became the third player to be sent off in an FA Cup final, after [[Kevin Moran (footballer)|Kevin Moran]] (in the [[1985 FA Cup final|1985 final]]) and [[José Antonio Reyes]] (in [[2005 FA Cup final|2005]]).<ref>{{cite web |first=Ben |last=Bloom |title=FA Cup final – Manchester City v Wigan Athletic: live |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/competitions/fa-cup/10051060/FA-Cup-final-Manchester-City-v-Wigan-Athletic-live.html |work=[[The Daily Telegraph]] |date=11 May 2013 |url-access=subscription |access-date=12 May 2013 |archive-date=12 May 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130512110803/http://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/competitions/fa-cup/10051060/FA-Cup-final-Manchester-City-v-Wigan-Athletic-live.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/mistake-that-almost-cost-moran-a-medal-1619799.html | work = [[The Independent]] | access-date = 5 October 2021 | title = Mistake that almost cost Moran a medal | first = Guy | last = Hodgson | date = 15 May 1995 | archive-date = 8 March 2016 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20160308015102/http://www.independent.co.uk/sport/mistake-that-almost-cost-moran-a-medal-1619799.html | url-access = registration | url-status = live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web | url = https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/fa_cup/4558271.stm | work = [[BBC Sport]] | access-date = 5 October 2021 | title = Arsenal 0–0 Man Utd (aet) | date = 21 May 2005 | archive-date = 14 August 2017 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20170814023916/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/fa_cup/4558271.stm | url-status = live }}</ref> As FA Cup winners, Wigan Athletic received £1.8&nbsp;million from the FA Cup Prize Fund, and Manchester City earned £900,000.<ref>{{cite web |title=2013 FA Cup Final |url=http://www.wembleystadium.com/Events/2013/The-FA-Cup-Final-2013/The-FA-Cup-Final-2013 |publisher =[[Wembley Stadium]] |date=11 May 2013 |access-date=24 April 2013 |archive-date=19 April 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140419015219/http://www.wembleystadium.com/Events/2013/The-FA-Cup-Final-2013/The-FA-Cup-Final-2013 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

[[Sporting Life (British newspaper)|''Sporting Life'']] described the win as the "biggest FA Cup final shock" since [[Wimbledon F.C.|Wimbledon]]'s defeat of [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]] in the [[1988 FA Cup final|1988 final]].<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.sportinglife.com/football/news/otd-wigan-stun-city-at-wembley/179812 | agency = [[Press Association]] | work = [[Sporting Life (British newspaper)|Sporting Life]] | access-date = 4 October 2021 | title = Wigan Athletic stun Manchester City to win the FA Cup for the first time in their history – on this day in 2013 | date = 11 May 2020 | archive-date = 4 October 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20211004151036/https://www.sportinglife.com/football/news/otd-wigan-stun-city-at-wembley/179812 | url-status = live }}</ref> Other commentators went further with [[Fox Sports]], [[BBC Sport]] and ''[[FourFourTwo]]'' all saying it was one of the biggest shocks in the competition's history, and [[bookmaker]] [[William Hill (bookmaker)|William Hill]] agreeing.<ref name=inside/><ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.foxsports.com/soccer/gallery/10-biggest-cinderella-runs-in-fa-cup-history-luton-bradford-wigan-sunderland-southampton-010617 | work = [[Fox Sports]] | access-date = 4 October 2021 | title = The 10 biggest Cinderella runs in FA Cup history | first = Thomas | last = Hautmann | date = 18 February 2017 | archive-date = 18 September 2017 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20170918050524/http://www.foxsports.com/soccer/gallery/10-biggest-cinderella-runs-in-fa-cup-history-luton-bradford-wigan-sunderland-southampton-010617 | url-status = live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.fourfourtwo.com/gallery/26-greatest-cup-shocks-english-football-history | work = [[FourFourTwo]] | access-date = 4 October 2021 | date = 11 January 2021 | title = The 26 greatest cup shocks in English football history | first = Alasdair | last = McKenzie | archive-date = 23 January 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210123232045/https://www.fourfourtwo.com/gallery/26-greatest-cup-shocks-english-football-history | url-status = live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web | url = https://news.williamhill.com/football/fa-cup-greatest-upsets-ever/ | date = 24 March 2021 | access-date = 4 October 2021 | title = The biggest FA Cup upsets in history | publisher = [[William Hill (bookmaker)|William Hill]] | archive-date = 24 January 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210124214610/https://news.williamhill.com/football/fa-cup-greatest-upsets-ever/ | url-status = live }}</ref> In a statistical analysis performed by ''[[Forbes]]'', the 2013 final was the tenth-biggest "surprise" result in the history of the FA Cup.<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.forbes.com/sites/jamesreade/2021/01/01/what-are-the-biggest-shocks-englands-fa-cup-has-ever-produced/ |archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210226012021/https://www.forbes.com/sites/jamesreade/2021/01/01/what-are-the-biggest-shocks-englands-fa-cup-has-ever-produced/ |archive-date=26 February 2021 | first =James | last= Reade | access-date = 4 October 2021 | title = What Are The Biggest Shocks England's FA Cup Has Ever Produced? | date = 1 January 2021 | work = [[Forbes]]}}</ref> European newspapers also commented on the shock result, ''[[La Gazzetta dello Sport]]'', ''[[Die Welt]]'' and ''[[Le Figaro]]'' all noting the historical significance of the event.<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://inthebox.gazzetta.it/2013/05/11/il-wigan-fa-la-storia-fa-cup-ai-latics | title = Il Wigan fa la storia: FA Cup ai Latics! | date = 11 May 2013 | first = Stefano | last = Cantalupi | trans-title = Wigan makes history: FA Cup at the Latics! |work = [[La Gazzetta dello Sport]] | language = Italian | access-date = 4 October 2021 | archive-date = 28 July 2013 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20130728040602/http://inthebox.gazzetta.it/2013/05/11/il-wigan-fa-la-storia-fa-cup-ai-latics/ | url-status = live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.welt.de/newsticker/sport-news/article116088894/Wigan-gewinnt-sensationell-FA-Cup-gegen-ManCity.html | language = German | work = [[Die Welt]] | access-date = 4 October 2021 | date = 11 May 2013 | trans-title = Wigan sensationally wins FA Cup against ManCity | title = Wigan gewinnt sensationell FA-Cup gegen ManCity | agency = [[Agence France-Presse]] | archive-date = 4 October 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20211004154053/https://www.welt.de/newsticker/sport-news/article116088894/Wigan-gewinnt-sensationell-FA-Cup-gegen-ManCity.html | url-status = live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.lefigaro.fr/sports/football/etranger/angleterre/actualites/city-tombe-de-haut-face-a-wigan-631481 | work = [[Le Figaro]] | first = Julien | last= Desbuissons |access-date = 4 October 2021 | date = 12 May 2013 | trans-title = City fall high against Wigan | language = French | title = City tombe de haut face à Wigan | archive-date = 4 October 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20211004154055/https://www.lefigaro.fr/sports/football/etranger/angleterre/actualites/city-tombe-de-haut-face-a-wigan-631481 | url-status = live }}</ref>

Wigan Athletic lost their next Premier League match, a 4–1 defeat against Arsenal at the [[Emirates Stadium]], and were consequently [[Promotion and relegation|relegated]] to the Championship. In doing so, they became the first club ever to have won the FA Cup and be relegated from the top tier of English football in the same season.<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/22430454 | work = [[BBC Sport]] | access-date = 24 June 2021 | title = Arsenal 4–1 Wigan | date = 14 May 2013 | archive-date = 6 March 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210306113126/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/22430454 | url-status = live }}</ref> Mancini was dismissed days after the final with the club owners citing that he had "failed to achieve any of the club's targets, with the exception of qualification for next season's Champions League".<ref name=mansack>{{Cite web | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/22507128 | title = Roberto Mancini: Manchester City sack manager | date = 14 May 2013 | work = [[BBC Sport]] | access-date = 24 June 2021 | archive-date = 2 May 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210502100938/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/22507128 | url-status = live }}</ref> His assistant, [[Brian Kidd]], took temporary charge of the club and they ended the season with a 2–0 victory at [[Reading F.C.|Reading]] followed by a 3–2 home defeat by [[Norwich City F.C.|Norwich City]] to finish second in the Premier League.<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/22430447 | first = Gary | last = Rose | access-date = 24 June 2021 | date = 14 May 2013 | work = [[BBC Sport]] | title = Reading 0–2 Manchester City | archive-date = 13 January 2020 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20200113083956/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/22430447 | url-status = live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/22499159 | first = Stephan | last = Shemilt | access-date = 24 June 2021 | date = 19 May 2013 | title = Manchester City 2–3 Norwich City | work = [[BBC Sport]] | archive-date = 12 November 2020 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20201112014607/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/22499159 | url-status = live }}</ref>

Because Manchester City and their semi-final opponent Chelsea had already qualified for the following season's [[2013–14 UEFA Champions League|Champions League]] by virtue of their league positions, Wigan Athletic had already gained a place in the group stage of the [[2013–14 UEFA Europa League|2013–14 Europa League]] competition by winning their semi-final match.<ref>{{cite web |title=FA Cup final to see Wigan write new chapter |url=https://www.uefa.com/news/0208-0f886bf36290-14e09838b206-1000--fa-cup-final-to-see-wigan-write-new-chapter/?referrer=%2Fmemberassociations%2Fassociation%3Deng%2Fnews%2Fnewsid%3D1941562 |publisher=[[UEFA]] |date=15 April 2013 |access-date=16 April 2013 |archive-date=1 May 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130501235027/http://www.uefa.com/memberassociations/association=eng/news/newsid=1941562.html |url-status=live }}</ref> They failed to progress to the knockout stage, finishing bottom of [[2013–14 UEFA Europa League group stage#Group D|Group D]].<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/25303137 | title = NK Maribor 2–1 Wigan Athletic | access-date = 12 July 2021 | work= [[BBC Sport]] | date = 12 December 2013 | first = Gary | last = Rose | archive-date = 20 October 2014 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20141020211436/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/25303137 | url-status = live }}</ref>

Pellegrini was appointed as the full-time replacement for Mancini in June 2013.<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/22909811 | title = Manuel Pellegrini: Manchester City appoint Chilean as manager | date = 14 June 2013 | access-date = 24 June 2021 | work = [[BBC Sport]] | archive-date = 18 January 2016 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20160118042011/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/22909811 | url-status = live }}</ref> Defender [[Joleon Lescott]] later reflected that "it was a little bit weird on the morning of the final. We were eating breakfast together and the TVs were on with Sky Sports News reporting that Mancini was going to get sacked".<ref name=inside>{{cite web | url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/43086897 | title=The inside story of Wigan's win over Manchester City in the 2013 final | date=11 May 2020 | first=Chris | last=Bevan | work=[[BBC Sport]] | access-date=24 June 2021 | archive-date=9 November 2020 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201109021524/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/43086897 | url-status=live }}</ref> In early June, Wigan's manager Martínez left the club and joined [[Everton F.C.|Everton]], being replaced at his former club by [[Owen Coyle]].<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.theguardian.com/football/2013/jun/05/roberto-martinez-everton-manager | work = [[The Guardian]] | access-date = 8 October 2021 | title = Roberto Martínez says his aim is to take Everton into the Champions League | first = Andy | last = Hunter | date = 5 June 2013 | archive-date = 26 May 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210526150813/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2013/jun/05/roberto-martinez-everton-manager | url-status = live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/22902197 | work= [[BBC Sport]] | access-date = 8 October 2021 | date = 14 June 2013 | title = Owen Coyle: Wigan Athletic name former Bolton boss as manager | archive-date = 14 June 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210614132014/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/22902197 | url-status = live }}</ref> The final drew a peak television audience of 9.4{{nbsp}}million.<ref name=times>{{Cite web | url = https://www.thetimes.com/sport/football/article/fa-cup-final-maintains-its-evening-slot-in-bbc-deal-sb2cllmb3fn | work = [[The Times]] | title = FA Cup Final maintains its evening slot in BBC deal | first = Gary | last = Jacob | access-date = 8 October 2021 | date = 18 July 2013 | archive-date = 8 October 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20211008145348/https://www.thetimes.co.uk/article/fa-cup-final-maintains-its-evening-slot-in-bbc-deal-sb2cllmb3fn | url-status = live }}</ref> Despite the criticism of the early evening kick-off time, the BBC confirmed that subsequent finals would be started at a similar time as part of their new deal to broadcast the final from 2014. However, the broadcaster agreed to schedule the match as the last game of the domestic season.<ref name=times/>

The clubs would meet in both domestic cups the following season at the [[City of Manchester Stadium]]; City defeated Wigan 5–0 in the [[2013–14 Football League Cup|League Cup]] third round en route to the [[2014 Football League Cup final|title]], before Wigan defeated City again in the sixth round of the [[2013–14 FA Cup|FA Cup]], winning 2–1,<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.11v11.com/matches/manchester-city-v-wigan-athletic-09-march-2014-307467/ | website = 11v11.com | publisher = Association of Football Statisticians | access-date = 12 July 2021 | title = Manchester City v Wigan Athletic, 09 March 2014 | archive-date = 12 July 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210712195120/https://www.11v11.com/matches/manchester-city-v-wigan-athletic-09-march-2014-307467/ | url-status = live }}</ref> but failed to retain the trophy after they lost in a [[penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalty shoot-out]] against eventual winners Arsenal in the semi-final.<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/26897266 | title = Wigan Athletic 1&ndash;1 Arsenal aet (2&ndash;4 on pens) | first = Phil | last = McNulty | authorlink = Phil McNulty | work= [[BBC Sport]] | access-date = 6 October 2021 | date = 12 April 2014 | archive-date = 28 June 2018 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20180628164123/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/26897266 | url-status = live }}</ref> They met for another FA Cup tie in the [[2017–18 FA Cup|2017–18 tournament]] where Wigan, then in [[EFL League One|League One]] (the [[English football league system|third tier of English football]]), won the fifth round tie 1–0 at the [[Brick Community Stadium|DW Stadium]],<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.11v11.com/matches/wigan-athletic-v-manchester-city-19-february-2018-356812/ | website = 11v11.com | publisher = Association of Football Statisticians| access-date = 12 July 2021 | title = Wigan Athletic v Manchester City, 19 February 2018 | archive-date = 12 July 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210712195122/https://www.11v11.com/matches/wigan-athletic-v-manchester-city-19-february-2018-356812/ | url-status = live }}</ref> which was described by BBC Sport as "one of the biggest FA Cup giant-killings".<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/43120219 | title = FA Cup: Just how big was Wigan's victory over Manchester City? | date = 20 February 2018 | work= [[BBC Sport]] | access-date = 4 October 2021 | archive-date = 13 June 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210613235255/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/43120219 | url-status = live }}</ref>

